<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wethersfield, Connecticut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1634">1634</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wethersfield is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> just south of Hartford, settled in 1634 by colonists from Massachusetts and one of the three river towns that formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> Colony. In 1781 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> and the Comte de Rochambeau planned the Yorktown campaign there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wethersfield-connecticut.jpg" alt="Wethersfield, Connecticut" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Webb_and_Isaac_Stevens_Houses_-_Wethersfield,_CT_-_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wethersfield is a town in Hartford County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, immediately south of Hartford along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a>. Colonial records spell the name Weathersfield and Wythersfield, and Native Americans called the place Pyquag. The colonists named it after Wethersfield, a village in the English county of Essex. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 27,298 people.</p><p>Its neighbourhood Old Wethersfield, added to the National Register of Historic Places in 1970, is the largest historic district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, covering two square miles and holding 1,100 buildings, many dating to the 17th, 18th and 19th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Puritan settlement party of ten men, among them John Oldham, Robert Seeley, Thomas Topping and Nathaniel Foote, founded the town in 1634, and it is arguably the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>. It was called Watertown until 21 February 1637, when it was incorporated with Windsor and Hartford. The three are the towns represented by the grapevines on the Flag of Connecticut. On 23 April 1637, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pequot-war">Pequot War</a>, the Wangunk chief Sequin attacked with Pequot help and killed six men and three women. Four witch trials and three executions took place in the 17th century, among them Mary Johnson in 1648.</p><p>From 1716 to 1718 the Collegiate School was briefly located there before moving to New Haven and becoming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale University</a>. In May 1781, at the Webb House, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> and the French general Rochambeau planned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-yorktown">Siege of Yorktown</a>. Silas Deane, envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, also lived in the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>For a century at least, Wethersfield was the centre of the onion trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, in the late 1700s and the early to middle 1800s, and outsiders called it Oniontown. In 1820 William G. Comstock founded what became Comstock, Ferre &amp; Company, described as America's oldest continuously operating seed company, which pioneered sealed packets of seeds. The Wethersfield Volunteer Fire Department, chartered on 12 May 1803, was the first formally chartered fire department in the state. The town was the setting of the novel The Witch of Blackbird Pond by Elizabeth George Speare.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wethersfield, Connecticut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1636 the minister Thomas Hooker led his congregation from Massachusetts to Hartford on the Connecticut River, joining settlers at Windsor and <b>Wethersfield</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/connecticut-colony">Connecticut Colony</a></li></ul></section>
